tem of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535477 17592186045443 true] [17592186265751 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 4x+y+2z=-3 \\\\  & y+3z=0 \\\\  & -z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=1\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-1$ and $y=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n4x+(3)+2(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{-3-3+2}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=3$, and $z=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535477 17592186045443 false] [17592186265751 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 4x+y+2z=-3 \\\\  & y+3z=0 \\\\  & -z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=1\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-1$ and $y=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n4x+(3)+2(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{-3-3+2}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=3$, and $z=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535477 17592186045443 true] [17592186265752 1074 "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n-{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3-2=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=-5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-(-5)+4(1)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{-1-5-4}{2}=-5.\n